The left-looming escape circuit (6 tool calls)                                 

Anatomy (real connectome)                                                      

 • LC4 (126 neurons, 71 L / 55 R) and LPLC2 (185, 94 L / 91 R) are the         
   looming-sensitive visual projection neurons, both purely excitatory, fed by 
   optic-lobe T2/TmY3/Tm4 (LC4) and T4/T5/Tm5f motion pathways (LPLC2).        
 • They synapse directly onto the escape descending neurons in the ipsilateral 
   hemisphere:                                                                 
    • LC4 → DNp04 (55% of DNp04's total input), → DNp02 (26%)                  
    • LPLC2 → DNp01 giant fiber (13% direct; the best indirect route via       
      PVLP151 is 40× weaker, score 0.003), → DNp04 (16%)                       
 • Both also feed a cloud of PVLP/AVLP interneurons (PVLP024 63%, PVLP111 73%, 
   PVLP069 78%) that presumably distribute the signal to the wider takeoff     
   program.                                                                    

Physiology (drive left LC4+LPLC2, 165 neurons, 0.8/step, 1 s)                  

                                                                    
 DN (left)                             baseline  stimulated  Δ      
 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
 DNp04                                 1 Hz      49 Hz       +48    
 DNp01 (giant fiber)                   1         48          +47    
 DNp02                                 1         47          +46    
 DNp11 / DNg40 / DNp03                 0–2       25          +23–25 
 DNp71, DNp05, DNpe056, DNp06, DNp103  0–2       16–20       +15–19 
                                                                    

The right-side homologs were essentially silent (DNp01 R +1, DNp02 R +1, DNp04 
R −1, DNp11 R 0). So a looming object on the left recruits the entire left     
escape DN ensemble — giant fiber (fast jump/wing-depressor takeoff),           
DNp02/DNp04/DNp11 (directional takeoff/leg-based pre-takeoff postural          
adjustment), DNp03/DNp06/DNp05 — with almost no crossover. Behaviorally this is
consistent with the fly's directional escape: ipsilateral DN activation biases 
the leg-push to jump away from the left stimulus. 674 neurons brain-wide       
changed >5 Hz.                                                                 

Scrambled control (degree-preserving) Same stimulus, same seed: DNp01 L Δ 0,   
DNp02 L Δ 0, DNp04 L Δ 0, DNp11 L Δ 0, DNp06 L Δ +2. Largest DN change anywhere
was ±3 Hz (noise), and only 166 neurons changed >5 Hz. The 45–48 Hz responses  
therefore depend on the specific LC4/LPLC2 → DNp wiring, not on these          
populations' size or synapse counts. Wiring restored to real connectome        
afterward.                                                                     

What the model cannot show                                                     

 • No real looming stimulus: I injected current into LC4/LPLC2 directly,       
   bypassing the retina, lamina, and T4/T5/Tm computations that make these     
   cells selective for expansion vs. other motion. The model says "if LC4/LPLC2
   fire, DNp01/02/04 fire," not that a looming disc would fire them.           
 • Drive is hand-calibrated (0.8/step drives most cells near threshold); the   
   saturating ~48 Hz values reflect the drive and the 20 ms step, not measured 
   escape latencies or the graded, timing-dependent LC4-vs-LPLC2 weighting that
   selects short-mode vs. long-mode takeoffs in vivo.                          
 • No plasticity, neuromodulation, or state (e.g., flight vs. standing,        
   habituation), and no ventral-nerve-cord/muscle readout: I can show which DNs
   fire, not the jump itself or its direction.                                 
 • Signs come from predicted neurotransmitters; type-level analysis hides      
   single-neuron heterogeneity (e.g., the retinotopic gradient of LC4 →        
   DNp02/DNp11 synapses that encodes stimulus azimuth).                        

